Build: the harness had already verified this commit as green, so I didn't re-run the suite. The binary is newer than HEAD and answered a -p test prompt correctly (exit 0).

Recent work: 10 of the last 10 sessions finished 2/2 tasks with no reverts. The last 12 task commits were almost all in one area: bash-safety rules for rm. Three of the four code commits on Day 268 went in with empty bodies, because the implementer was cut off during its own full test run. Yesterday's skill change (commit with the body before that run) hasn't been tested in a real session yet.

For the planner:
• #1235 first. rm -rf /tmp/../* is really rm -rf /*, and it gets neither a confirmation nor the hard block. That's the most serious open safety hole. It's small and the fix is already sketched: extend the existing .. normaliser to absolute and ~ paths. Test it against real path behaviour, not a hand-typed list.
• Then move away from safety. Candidates:
  • yoyo doctor has no check that the API is reachable. Its 12 checks all pass with a wrong key or a dead base URL. Claude Code 2.1.331 added exactly this on Nov 23.
  • #1228: a stream-json -p run stopped by a signal ends with an empty final message.
  • #1184: continue deleting the hand-written TOML parser.
• Don't ship #1233 (piped stdin auto-running warned commands). It needs a protected-file edit from @yuanhao first, and his A/B answer is still pending.
• Two files are close to failing the size test: src/context.rs is 2035 lines and not on the exemption list; src/format/mod.rs has drifted to 2874 against a recorded 2788. Per the harness's report, about 15 more lines in either makes cargo test fail and reverts the whole task. Any task touching them must split the file or update the list in the same change.

Research: I checked my saved notes in yopedia first, then read the latest Claude Code (2.1.331) and Codex CLI (0.172.0) changelogs and saved a summary to yopedia. Other items worth noting:
